Thanks, Yago, for the suggestions.

When I tried using c*1 to constrain the intercept, I got the same results (the intercepts for Post1, Post2, etc. are all near 0) as when I used c*1, C*2, etc. along  with the same error message.

When I tried the second method you mentioned by leaving out c, the intercepts of the latent variables Post1, Post2, etc. no longer appear in the output. The error message disappeared, though.

My purpose is to obtain the intercepts of the latent variables at time2. But the analyses so far either gave me a zero intercept or the intercept does not show.

On Tuesday, February 27, 2024 at 7:53:57 AM UTC-6 yagol...@gmail.com wrote:
>
> I guess lavaan cannot understand this part
> Post1 ~ c*1 + b1*Pre1 Post2 ~ c*2 + b2*Pre2 Post3 ~ c*3 + b3*Pre3 Post4 ~ c*4 + b4*Pre4 Post5 ~ c*5 + b5*Pre5 Post6 ~ c*6 + b6*Pre6
>
> The letter ‘c’ is a label that constrains all parameters that it precedes to be equal, but there is no parameter 2, 3, …, 6.
> If you want to constrain the intercept to be equal in cases, it should be
> Post1 ~ c*1 + b1*Pre1 Post2 ~ c*1 + b2*Pre2 . . . Post6 ~ c*1 + b6*Pre6
>
> If you want to freely estimate them, I guess you can just take out the 'c'.
>
> Best,
>
> Yago
